-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** The pseudocode clamps coordinates to the volume bounds. Alternative methods include wrapping or mirroring, which may affect the visual result near edges [G].
-*   **Precision:** Using floating-point arithmetic for the convolution is recommended to avoid quantization artifacts [G].
-*   **Kernel Size:** Larger tap counts (e.g., 13-tap) provide a better Gaussian approximation but increase computational cost [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** The source does not specify boundary handling (e.g., clamping, wrapping, or reflection) for lookups outside the cube bounds [S1]. Implementers must define this behavior [G].
+*   **Precision:** The coefficients are given to high precision; standard floating-point precision is assumed sufficient [S1].
+*   **Anisotropy:** The source notes that while isotropic kernels are considered for simplicity, anisotropic kernels could use a similar solution [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The tap-filter algorithm has a complexity of $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of lookups (voxels processed) [S1]. In conjunction with ray-tracing, the total algorithm complexity becomes $O(NMD)$, where $M$ is the number of voxels visited by a ray, $N$ is the number of lookups, and $D$ is the dimensionality of the convolution kernel (1-D, 2-D, 3-D, ..., N-D) [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$, where $V$ is the number of voxels in the volume, as intermediate results are stored for each axis pass [G].
-*   **Performance:** The method is suitable for real-time rendering. Tests showed a minor slowdown in frame rate when using this technique compared to non-smoothed rendering, but it remains efficient due to the separability of the filter [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides smooth, artifact-free results; computationally efficient due to separability; easy to implement in shaders [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Blurs edges and fine details; not suitable for preserving sharp boundaries (unlike Bilateral Filter) [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When smooth visualization of volumetric data is required and edge preservation is not critical.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When sharp boundaries or high-frequency details must be preserved.
+*   **Complexity:** The tap-filter algorithm has a complexity of $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of lookups [S1]. In conjunction with ray-tracing, the total algorithm complexity becomes $O(NMD)$, where $M$ is the number of voxels visited by a ray, $N$ is the number of lookups, and $D$ is the dimensionality of the convolution kernel [S1].
+*   **Texture Fetches:** For an N-tap 3D Gaussian kernel, the number of texture fetches per step is $3 \times \frac{N-1}{2} + 1$ [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Kernel size (e.g., 9-tap) affects rendering speed, but because $M$ and $D$ are small in typical implementations, the slowdown is minor and manageable by modern graphics hardware [S1]. Benchmarks on a 1024³ cube showed that without smoothing, frame rates were ~2 fps on some GPUs, while smoothing added a minor slowdown [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides smoother transitions than box filters and effectively denoises spectral cubes, especially when rays traverse the cube at an angle [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The smoothing effect is purely spatial; it does not preserve edges based on intensity values like the Bilateral Filter [S1].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Smoothing using the tap-filter algorithm was introduced in the context of real-time volume rendering with graphics shaders [S1]. The method leverages the approximation of Gaussian distributions by cascaded convolution of box filters, a technique attributed to Crowley et al. (2002) [S1]. The calculation of coefficients and lookup coordinates for the binomial filter is based on formulas presented by Daniel Rákos [S1]. This approach is particularly useful in medical imaging and scientific visualization where real-time performance is essential [S1].
+The method is described in the context of real-time colouring and filtering of astronomical spectral cubes using graphics shaders [S1]. It builds on the work of Crowley et al. (2002) regarding cascaded convolution of $[1,1]$ kernels and Daniel Rákos's blog post on efficient Gaussian blur with linear sampling, which proposed using bilinear texture filtering to reduce fetch counts [S1]. It is used in radio astronomy visualization (e.g., Antennae cube) to improve the visual quality of Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) and Average Intensity Volume Projection (AVIP) renderings [S1].
 
